WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.462
- People don't understand the
feeling of going to school,

00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:10.427
and you sit next to a friend, and that
same night, you hearing that friend was

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:16.099
murdered and you sitting in that same
desk that next day, and he's not there,

00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:18.059
and you realize he’s not coming back here.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.395
He didn't transfer to another school.

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:23.356
He didn't—he wasn't sick.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.108
Like, he's not coming back, period.

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:27.861
- [Clapping]
- OK.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:28.528
That good?

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:29.863
- That was a terrible cut, but—
- [Laughing]

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.197
- All right.

00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:33.533
- All right, you ready, DeMar?
- Yeah.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:33.575
- I'm Taylor Rooks, and these are
conversations about defining one's

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:37.829
- I'm Taylor Rooks, and these are
conversations about defining one's

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:41.708
relationship to Blackness and
how that defines their America.

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:43.877
OK, DeMar, same question

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:47.172
I ask everybody first on this series:

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:50.759
When did you realize that being
Black was different from being white?

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:54.179
- Playing basketball always gave me
the opportunity to see different

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:55.805
places, be around different places.

00:00:56.514 --> 00:01:01.394
And I think the first time, I think I was
in ninth grade. We would—we drove out,

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:04.355
like, two hours away to this school,

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.067
this white community,

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:13.198
and I've seen for the first time
how different their schools were,

00:01:13.531 --> 00:01:18.078
facilities, just everything about

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.331
that culture was like
night and day from us.

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:25.877
And it used to—from then on,
it used to make me question, like,

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:28.463
“Damn, why, why’s their stuff better?

00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:29.589
Why they have this better?

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:30.799
Why they talk better?

00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:33.176
Or why they dress better?

00:01:33.176 --> 00:01:37.972
Or why—” So many questions came about,
you know, and as I continue to get older

00:01:37.972 --> 00:01:43.186
and get older, I’ve seen the separation
from, you know, privilege, privilege,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:49.776
to, you know, where we came from, you know,
getting everything, you know, feeling like

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:51.736
leftovers and making it work, you know?
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:57.367
- Um, that's when I first started noticing it.
- When you noticed it, that disparity

00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:01.204
and like the, just the huge, huge,
difference, how did that make you feel?

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:04.374
- It leaves you numb for a while

00:02:04.666 --> 00:02:07.794
for me personally because you
was just trying to figure it out, you know?

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:13.716
And as I became older and started
to see things differently,

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:18.263
you know, it just always left a
question mark with a lot of things,

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:24.185
you know? And some I never even told
people out, out loud was when

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:28.314
I went to college, even though I went
to SC that was 15, 20 minutes away

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:29.983
from Compton, I had a culture shock.

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:34.946
Like, it was, it was the first time
that I was really consistently

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:37.699
around, you know, white people.

00:02:38.449 --> 00:02:40.034
And you seeing

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:44.789
everybody with laptops,
talking with a different type of

00:02:44.789 --> 00:02:48.293
vernacular. Like, the slang was out.

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:52.297
Like, it was so many things that was so
different from my culture, and it was

00:02:52.297 --> 00:02:57.302
kinda intimidating, you know?
- [Taylor:] Yeah.
- It was kind of intimidating because that was like the

00:02:57.302 --> 00:02:59.095
first time where I'm in that environment

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:02.557
that, that didn't feel like
me or feel comfortable.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:06.769
And I felt like they was looking
at me differently other than

00:03:06.978 --> 00:03:08.563
just being an athlete, you know?

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:12.150
And it—that, that was kind
of a frustrating adjustment that

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.693
I had to get used to in college.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.403
- How did you get used to it?

00:03:16.237 --> 00:03:17.113
- It took a while.

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:20.867
Um, I didn't live—I didn't
stay on campus for my first

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.160
three, four months of college.

00:03:22.744 --> 00:03:25.205
- Did you not stay on campus
because of the culture shock?

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:25.622
- Yeah.

00:03:25.622 --> 00:03:29.250
It was just too much of a culture
shock, like, being around it.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:36.257
And it was like, it was like, it
was a feeling that is so hard to

00:03:36.257 --> 00:03:42.180
explain because I grew up in this
urban environment, and I felt like

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.516
that was the norm to go into that.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.018
I couldn't imagine being—

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:53.524
going to University of Kentucky being
from L.A.. You know, being left out, trying

00:03:53.524 --> 00:03:56.486
to figure this out on my own, but

00:03:57.278 --> 00:03:57.946
it was rough.

00:03:58.029 --> 00:03:59.322
It was, it was extremely rough.

00:03:59.322 --> 00:04:01.950
And I just wanted to stay in my
element as long as possible

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:05.870
till I felt comfortable being on campus.
- Really?
- [DeMar:] Yeah.

00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:15.797
- So when you kinda decided to go back to
campus and be around, you know, everybody

00:04:15.797 --> 00:04:19.384
that was on campus, do you feel nervous?

00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:20.927
Do you feel like you won't be accepted?

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:24.013
Like, what's the hesitation there?
- Yeah, being nervous. Not accepted.

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:33.022
Not having what they have. Not, you know,
not knowledgeable of,

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:36.150
of the freedom of movement that they have.

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:41.531
You know, people always think it's cool
for us as athletes, just to be an athlete.

00:04:41.572 --> 00:04:44.993
But at the end of the day, we’re people.
- [Taylor:] Yeah.
- We’re human beings,

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:46.911
and we wanna be accepted that way.

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:53.584
So for me not to be able to comprehend
with a different culture and

00:04:53.584 --> 00:04:57.755
knowing that my culture are
looked down upon in

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:58.423
a certain type of way

00:04:59.507 --> 00:05:00.258
was frustrating.

00:05:00.258 --> 00:05:02.593
So I shied away from it for a long time.

00:05:03.761 --> 00:05:10.810
- You know, I wonder if we underestimate
the challenge that Black athletes

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.020
have going to white universities.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.064
And honestly, I don't know if I ever
really sat and thought about it in

00:05:15.064 --> 00:05:19.152
that way, cuz I'm seeing so much
of it from my experience. But a lot of

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:21.237
these athletes that go and play sports

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:25.241
are coming from areas where they
probably have not interacted

00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:26.242
with a lot of white people.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:26.951
- Right.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:27.493
Right.

00:05:27.618 --> 00:05:33.541
You know, I think, I think that definitely
goes unnoticed because it's,

00:05:33.541 --> 00:05:35.543
it's one of those things to where it's

00:05:36.627 --> 00:05:42.091
just college in itself is different. It's
hard for us being minorities coming from

00:05:42.091 --> 00:05:46.554
where we come from to all of a sudden,
we’re sitting in these intimidating

00:05:46.554 --> 00:05:50.975
classrooms with a different
type of culture that don't know us,

00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:55.396
don't get, get us, you
know? And it's never been—

00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:59.025
it's just always been an
acceptance factor, not feeling

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:00.568
like we was always accepted

00:06:02.236 --> 00:06:06.616
unless we was an athlete or someone of,
or something instead of just

00:06:06.616 --> 00:06:08.451
being looked at as a person.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:09.327
- Yeah.

00:06:09.577 --> 00:06:11.913
And I think you said a key
thing that I think is important.

00:06:11.913 --> 00:06:12.663
It's not

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:17.126
about you accepting them. It's that you
feel like they won’t accept you.

00:06:17.168 --> 00:06:17.418
- Right. Right. Exactly.

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.129
- And those two things really,
really aren't the same.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.010
You know, I was really glad that I am
able to talk to you because honestly, when

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:32.225
I saw that you protested in California,
you were super heavy on my heart because

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:37.688
I thought about when I interviewed you
for my show, we were talking about death.
- Mmhmm.

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:44.987
- And you said that you were almost desensitized to it.
- Mmhmm.

00:06:44.987 --> 00:06:48.449
- The idea of people dying was something that had
made you numb cuz you had seen it so much.

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:53.538
So what is it like for you to see
Black people be murdered by police?

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:02.004
- I mean, it’s...it's a thing that, like, even with
this last situation—you know, rest in

00:07:02.004 --> 00:07:06.050
peace to George Floyd—
when that situation happened and,

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:07.301
you know, I started seeing

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:11.305
all the protests, all, you know,
the looting, you just started

00:07:11.305 --> 00:07:13.349
to see everything transpire.

00:07:13.975 --> 00:07:16.018
I remember I called my mom one night.

00:07:16.352 --> 00:07:19.188
I asked her, I said, I was, I
think I was three years old

00:07:19.188 --> 00:07:20.273
when the Watts riot happened,

00:07:20.481 --> 00:07:23.359
when the Rodney King beating happened.
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:26.863
- And I just wanted to ask her how,

00:07:27.447 --> 00:07:29.198
“What was I doing at three years old?”

00:07:29.365 --> 00:07:32.201
You know, cuz especially me, I
have a three-year-old daughter

00:07:32.201 --> 00:07:33.870
and a seven-year-old daughter.

00:07:34.245 --> 00:07:38.416
And I remember one night, I had the
news on, I'm seeing all the fires

00:07:38.416 --> 00:07:39.917
and all the buildings on fire.

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:43.629
And then I see my kids sitting
there, and they’re really not knowing

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:44.881
and understanding what's going on.

00:07:44.881 --> 00:07:49.969
So I put myself in their shoes, and I
remember I called my mom, and I asked myself

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.642
what was going on. And it gave
me a perspective on what type of

00:07:56.767 --> 00:08:02.190
lifestyle I was living and grew
up in, of that being the norm.

00:08:02.565 --> 00:08:06.027
And it was more so like,
“Yeah, you were sitting there

00:08:06.027 --> 00:08:08.821
playing.” But, you know, so much

00:08:09.238 --> 00:08:14.452
after that transpired and became worse
and worse in my lifetime till it made me feel

00:08:14.952 --> 00:08:20.541
these senseless ties to certain tragedies
because, you know, you gotta keep going.

00:08:20.541 --> 00:08:23.503
You gotta keep moving. You gotta keep
pushing, you know? And it

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:26.631
was one of those things to where, you
know, they didn't have it figured

00:08:26.631 --> 00:08:29.008
out, but it was more so like,
“Yeah, that's what the police do.”

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:30.051
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:08:30.051 --> 00:08:34.263
- So growing up, it was more so
me knowing and feeling like,

00:08:34.305 --> 00:08:36.390
“Oh, that's the norm,” or “The police,

00:08:36.807 --> 00:08:40.311
oh, they just can't wait to catch a
Black, Black man doing something wrong

00:08:40.311 --> 00:08:41.896
so they could do this to you.”

00:08:42.021 --> 00:08:46.150
You know what I mean?
- [Taylor:] Yeah.
- This is way before cameras and all this filming stuff was going on.

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:47.693
It was always, like, growing up was

00:08:49.237 --> 00:08:52.907
taught to me was, like, “By all means,

00:08:52.907 --> 00:08:53.991
if you see the police,

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.786
let them pass by, then
go about your business.”

00:08:56.786 --> 00:09:00.456
You know, it gave me an
uncomfortable factor because

00:09:00.456 --> 00:09:03.292
growing up, it was so normal

00:09:04.168 --> 00:09:08.214
to think and fear the police because
you didn't wanna end up, like, in

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:09.632
a situation like that with them.
You know what I mean?

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:11.050
- Yeah.

00:09:11.342 --> 00:09:16.013
As, as this has happened, I think
people are seeing that Black

00:09:16.347 --> 00:09:19.767
people aren't surprised that it's
happening because we grow up,

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:24.313
we grew up with these talks of
what you do when you see a cop and

00:09:24.313 --> 00:09:26.440
that a lot of cops will hurt you.

00:09:26.774 --> 00:09:26.941
- Right.

00:09:26.941 --> 00:09:28.818
- So it isn't an element of surprise.

00:09:28.818 --> 00:09:31.237
It's more of like, “Oh, this happened again.”
- Right.

00:09:31.821 --> 00:09:31.946
Yeah.

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:34.949
- And I think a lot of trauma comes with that.

00:09:35.366 --> 00:09:39.537
You know, knowing that if a white
person sees a cop, they feel safe.
- [DeMar:] Yep.

00:09:40.329 --> 00:09:43.958
- I don't think I've ever seen a cop
and been like, “Oh, like, I'm safe.”

00:09:44.333 --> 00:09:46.669
- [DeMar:] Right.
- I'm like, “Oh, I gotta go in another lane.”

00:09:47.628 --> 00:09:48.546
- [DeMar:] Yeah.
- You know what I mean?

00:09:49.297 --> 00:09:53.759
And that's a conversation that
I think I'm glad is being had now.

00:09:54.218 --> 00:09:54.760
- [DeMar:] Mmhmm.

00:09:55.052 --> 00:10:00.099
- Just the way that we all grow up
and our relationship to authority

00:10:00.349 --> 00:10:03.436
and knowing that—when I say
authority, I mean cops, but I also

00:10:03.436 --> 00:10:06.188
mean white people—
- [DeMar:] Yeah.
- And the privilege and power that white people have.

00:10:06.564 --> 00:10:10.735
Our relationship to that authority is kind of coming to the forefront.
- Mmhmm.

00:10:11.861 --> 00:10:18.534
- You also said something in our interview
that you had to grow up very quickly.

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:23.331
And you had to understand things that
a kid isn't supposed to understand.

00:10:23.956 --> 00:10:28.502
What are some things that you were referring to?
- Death. You know,

00:10:28.586 --> 00:10:34.258
early on, at five, six years old,
I lost my uncle to gang violence.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.927
I lost another uncle to gang violence.

00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:41.807
I remember going to
funerals and, you know, seeing my mother

00:10:42.475 --> 00:10:49.065
cry countless nights, countless days
to where, you know, I remember one year

00:10:49.607 --> 00:10:56.364
my mama went to 15, 20 funerals, you
know? And it got to a point to where to

00:10:56.364 --> 00:11:04.830
this day, I...I don't like funerals
because they were so...they came up so

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:10.086
frequently through, through my youth,
you know, and that was something to where

00:11:12.254 --> 00:11:15.466
people don't understand the
feeling of going to school,

00:11:16.509 --> 00:11:22.473
and you sit next to a friend, and that
same night, you hearing that friend was

00:11:22.473 --> 00:11:28.145
murdered and you sitting in that same
desk that next day, and he's not there,

00:11:28.187 --> 00:11:30.106
and you realize he’s not coming back here.

00:11:30.731 --> 00:11:32.441
He didn't transfer to another school.

00:11:32.983 --> 00:11:35.403
He didn't—he wasn't sick.

00:11:35.403 --> 00:11:37.154
Like, he's not coming back, period.

00:11:37.446 --> 00:11:37.780
You know?

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.742
To deal with that and
to comprehend with that,

00:11:42.159 --> 00:11:45.788
I think people really don't understand
unless you go through it, how hard

00:11:45.788 --> 00:11:49.542
it is. And it puts you in a space to
where either it could break you down,

00:11:49.959 --> 00:11:52.753
or you going to build up
this wall inside of you

00:11:53.254 --> 00:11:56.257
that gives you the extra
strength to keep pushing and

00:11:56.257 --> 00:12:00.094
just wanna make it out and not
be in that position anymore to keep

00:12:00.094 --> 00:12:01.303
dealing with this type of feeling.

00:12:01.512 --> 00:12:04.932
You know, I lost my best
friend in high school

00:12:06.142 --> 00:12:08.894
to murder. You know, all these
things to where I haven't

00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:12.815
even had a developmental yet,

00:12:13.315 --> 00:12:19.280
and I'm comprehending the worst thing
possible that a kid could go through.

00:12:19.572 --> 00:12:27.747
So with that, it's definitely something that—that definitely leaves, leaves,

00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:29.832
a scar inside of you for a long time.

00:12:29.832 --> 00:12:30.166
So,

00:12:30.666 --> 00:12:31.751
um, it was tough.

00:12:31.876 --> 00:12:36.422
Like I said, you know, I've
been an advocate for, you know, depression.

00:12:36.881 --> 00:12:39.967
And a lot of that stems
from my, from my childhood—

00:12:40.301 --> 00:12:41.677
things that I never addressed.

00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:45.181
And, you know, I think at this
point of time going on in the

00:12:45.181 --> 00:12:48.184
world, there's so much going on.

00:12:48.267 --> 00:12:51.228
It's so much. It's
definitely traumatizing.

00:12:51.353 --> 00:12:54.106
You know, when you turn on the news,
and you see so much negativity. You

00:12:54.106 --> 00:12:58.486
seeing the police do this to us over
and over and over, and you seeing

00:12:58.527 --> 00:13:03.741
innocent people being killed over and
over and over. You know, it's just, it's

00:13:03.741 --> 00:13:08.704
just a time that, that—an opportunity
for us to all come together, not just

00:13:08.704 --> 00:13:11.791
celebrities, people in general, and voice

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:15.795
how they feel and their opinion
and really make a change.

00:13:16.212 --> 00:13:19.298
- You talk about the trauma of like
turning on the TV and seeing bad

00:13:19.298 --> 00:13:22.092
things and be looking at your phone
and seeing Black people being murdered.

00:13:22.718 --> 00:13:24.178
- Mmhmm.
- When I've been talking to Black people,

00:13:24.178 --> 00:13:27.056
I've really just been asking, like, "Are you OK?"
- Right, right.

00:13:27.973 --> 00:13:29.683
- So, I mean, are you OK,

00:13:29.683 --> 00:13:30.226
really?

00:13:31.185 --> 00:13:34.939
- Yeah, it's tough. In my mind it's
like, how can we be OK?

00:13:35.481 --> 00:13:39.860
You know, and I asked myself,
something the other day on TV, and I was

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:45.282
just like, it was a newscaster, she
was talking to a Black man,

00:13:45.282 --> 00:13:46.992
and he was expressing his frustration.

00:13:47.326 --> 00:13:50.704
And through his frustration, you know,
he was using cuss words here and there.

00:13:51.163 --> 00:13:55.251
And the newscaster on TV was
like, "Oh, we gotta cut him off.

00:13:55.251 --> 00:13:56.669
He using too much profanity."

00:13:57.461 --> 00:14:00.881
And in my mind, I'm sitting there
watching and I'm like, "But you could

00:14:00.881 --> 00:14:02.716
pick up your phone, and we seeing

00:14:03.717 --> 00:14:06.720
officers killing unarmed people.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:12.309
So you, you undermining
how the way this man expressed his

00:14:12.309 --> 00:14:14.436
frustration because he tired of seeing

00:14:15.145 --> 00:14:17.773
people die for nothing?"

00:14:18.357 --> 00:14:20.442
And it was like, "Really?"

00:14:20.442 --> 00:14:21.861
That just shows me right there

00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:24.113
you don't know what it's like
to walk in our shoes, to be a

00:14:24.113 --> 00:14:26.198
Black person. You know what I mean?
- [Taylor:] Mmhmm.

00:14:26.198 --> 00:14:27.825
- You worried about him saying a cuss word?

00:14:28.701 --> 00:14:30.035
You should be more worried about

00:14:30.536 --> 00:14:33.831
being able to go to your phone and
seeing so many people killed for nothing.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:38.002
Like that's what's...that's the
type of stuff that trips me out.

00:14:38.085 --> 00:14:38.794
You know what I mean?

00:14:38.836 --> 00:14:41.213
And it's like, how can we be OK with that,

00:14:41.547 --> 00:14:47.636
when you seeing that every day or, or
you don't see people sympathizing

00:14:47.928 --> 00:14:49.054
what it's like to be Black?

00:14:49.179 --> 00:14:49.847
You know what I mean?

00:14:49.847 --> 00:14:53.517
- Absolutely.
- Like, that's my frustration.

00:14:53.601 --> 00:14:57.062
And I don't think my
frustration will change until

00:14:57.605 --> 00:15:02.109
people could get some sort of idea and
sympathize with that and start to begin

00:15:02.359 --> 00:15:02.776
to help

00:15:03.444 --> 00:15:05.029
with us as a collective group.

00:15:05.029 --> 00:15:08.282
That's the only way, you know,
other—if you don't do that, it's

00:15:08.282 --> 00:15:09.742
going to be that frustration.

00:15:09.742 --> 00:15:12.202
It's going to be that aggression,
you know, because that's all, that's

00:15:12.202 --> 00:15:15.080
all we know. Especially when it's
something that we're passionate about,

00:15:15.456 --> 00:15:16.749
and we're passionate about our people.

00:15:16.749 --> 00:15:20.002
So, we gotta take care of
that, and the best way to do it

00:15:20.002 --> 00:15:21.337
is we all come together and do it

00:15:22.212 --> 00:15:26.300
with every race.
- And you make a good point with this white woman's

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.386
reaction to the Black man is, white

00:15:29.386 --> 00:15:32.389
people cannot police
how we feel right now.

00:15:32.932 --> 00:15:33.182
- Right.

00:15:33.641 --> 00:15:40.356
- Not only because they'll never understand
it, but because the only thing that

00:15:40.356 --> 00:15:43.817
we have to give is the emotion.
- [DeMar:] Right.

00:15:43.859 --> 00:15:46.445
- It's the emotion that's
carrying us through.

00:15:47.071 --> 00:15:50.032
It's the emotion that
makes protests successful.

00:15:50.407 --> 00:15:52.701
It's the emotion that brings change.

00:15:53.118 --> 00:15:55.496
So the more a white person
feels uncomfortable with

00:15:55.496 --> 00:15:57.039
how we express ourselves,

00:15:57.039 --> 00:16:00.042
it's really just them saying they're
uncomfortable with the movement.

00:16:00.292 --> 00:16:00.584
- Right.

00:16:00.793 --> 00:16:01.085
Right. That's all it is.

00:16:02.127 --> 00:16:02.461
- Yeah.

00:16:02.503 --> 00:16:03.253
We need that.

00:16:03.504 --> 00:16:04.004
It's,

00:16:04.213 --> 00:16:08.592
it's our biggest power right now, is each
other and how we're looking after each

00:16:08.592 --> 00:16:10.636
other and how those emotions manifest.

00:16:10.636 --> 00:16:14.348
So I understand why you'd be frustrated
to hear that. I'm frustrated.

00:16:14.515 --> 00:16:17.768
I'm frustrated to even hear that
a woman tried to police his, his

00:16:17.768 --> 00:16:19.520
words when we're trying to change

00:16:19.979 --> 00:16:24.400
people's actions. You know, the
words are the base level of it.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:30.114
So, yeah, I think a lot of Black
people—like, you know, I had a thought

00:16:31.615 --> 00:16:35.619
when someone had asked me if I was
OK, my thought was like, "If I'm

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:39.123
killed, will the person be arrested?"

00:16:39.581 --> 00:16:39.873
- Yeah.

00:16:39.915 --> 00:16:44.586
That's—it's all these questions
that everybody that I see asks

00:16:44.586 --> 00:16:48.090
are legitimate questions that we
still cannot get an answer to.
- Mmhmm.

00:16:48.674 --> 00:16:57.224
- But you wonder why we're frustrated, get upset,
and want to question our actions. You know? I'm, like, come on.

00:16:57.266 --> 00:16:59.059
Not, not just with this situation.

00:16:59.643 --> 00:17:02.813
A year ago, 10 years ago, 20.
This has been going on.

00:17:03.188 --> 00:17:03.689
You know what I mean?

00:17:03.689 --> 00:17:04.481
It's been going on.

00:17:04.481 --> 00:17:06.025
Like, I grew up seeing it, and

00:17:06.025 --> 00:17:07.192
I'm 30 years old.

00:17:07.234 --> 00:17:10.946
I can imagine how—my
dad is from Louisiana.

00:17:11.196 --> 00:17:15.534
Like he grew up in whole
segregated South and everything.

00:17:15.534 --> 00:17:21.248
So up until I was 12 years old, he
used to take me to Louisiana with him.

00:17:22.124 --> 00:17:22.666
All the time.

00:17:22.666 --> 00:17:26.837
Like, once every summer till I was 12
years old, and I didn't really understand

00:17:26.837 --> 00:17:34.928
it until I got older and I start to see
the segregation of how it was in the South, and

00:17:35.596 --> 00:17:40.726
the obstacles my dad had to go through,
had to deal with, had to—it's like,

00:17:41.226 --> 00:17:41.977
all these things.

00:17:41.977 --> 00:17:45.481
And I used to always question, like,
me being from the city, being a city

00:17:45.481 --> 00:17:48.567
boy, I used to hate going through
the country, but that was one of the

00:17:48.567 --> 00:17:55.074
lessons that showed me so much as a
kid, because it was, it's like, I couldn't imagine

00:17:55.074 --> 00:17:58.077
growing up in that. I couldn't imagine growing up.
- Yeah.
- You know what I mean?

00:17:58.118 --> 00:17:58.994
Like, I couldn't.

00:17:59.495 --> 00:18:01.580
We think, we think it's crazy now.

00:18:01.914 --> 00:18:06.627
Like it's, it's, our parents and
grandparents went through so

00:18:06.627 --> 00:18:09.213
much, and now I look at it

00:18:09.213 --> 00:18:15.677
like this is an ultimate opportunity for
us to, to do something incredible, like,

00:18:16.011 --> 00:18:17.930
incredible, and really make a change.
- Yeah.

00:18:19.431 --> 00:18:23.852
How has your mental health
been throughout this?

00:18:24.311 --> 00:18:26.105
- Um, I've been managing it.

00:18:26.105 --> 00:18:32.236
I think, I think for me, just being more
mature and understanding how to

00:18:32.236 --> 00:18:36.782
reach out and speak to other individuals
that may not know how to handle—

00:18:37.157 --> 00:18:37.491
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:18:37.533 --> 00:18:42.246
- Things has been helpful for me. You
know, before it, I used to close

00:18:42.246 --> 00:18:44.248
in and become sheltered and kinda

00:18:44.998 --> 00:18:49.419
steer away and shy away from
everything, you know, and try

00:18:49.419 --> 00:18:50.587
to figure it out on my own.

00:18:50.712 --> 00:18:53.966
I think more so for me, I've been more so,

00:18:55.259 --> 00:18:59.596
wanna lend a hand, and that's
been therapeutic for me to be

00:18:59.596 --> 00:19:01.890
able to have others listen to me.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:06.270
And I give whatever,
experience and whatever honesty that I'm

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:08.147
dealing with and try to share that.

00:19:08.355 --> 00:19:11.150
You know, I think that's been
definitely beneficial for me.

00:19:11.441 --> 00:19:14.111
Especially without having, you know, basketball all this time.
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:19:14.903 --> 00:19:16.947
- So that's been something
that's been helpful to me.

00:19:17.614 --> 00:19:20.993
- One thing that I think, you know,
I admire about you and a lot of the

00:19:20.993 --> 00:19:23.495
Black men that I end up speaking to

00:19:24.454 --> 00:19:29.710
is you find a lot of power and vulnerability—
- Mmhmm.
- And understanding that

00:19:30.127 --> 00:19:34.965
emotion can really help you. But
there's also a conversation to be had

00:19:34.965 --> 00:19:39.887
about Black men growing up feeling
as though they can't be emotional.

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:40.220
- Right.

00:19:40.512 --> 00:19:42.514
- Was that your experience?

00:19:42.681 --> 00:19:46.310
- And if it was your experience,
how did you shift away from that?

00:19:52.024 --> 00:19:57.029
- I grew up in Compton. It was a tough time, man, like,
it was a tough time because every
single day was worse than the day

00:19:57.321 --> 00:20:03.744
previous, you know?
- [Taylor:] Mmhmm.
- And it became such a normal, it became such a normal, normal time.

00:20:03.827 --> 00:20:06.121
You know, I talked to a lot of
my friends that I grew up with,

00:20:06.788 --> 00:20:09.041
and we really sit back and

00:20:09.833 --> 00:20:14.254
trip on how crazy the times were,
but we didn't look at it like

00:20:14.254 --> 00:20:15.964
that because that's all we knew.

00:20:16.215 --> 00:20:16.757
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:20:17.883 --> 00:20:24.348
- You know, for me, what helped me was
when I had my first daughter. That showed

00:20:24.348 --> 00:20:26.975
me a sense of empathy, compassion,

00:20:27.684 --> 00:20:32.272
love and care and a whole element of
something that I never, I never really

00:20:32.272 --> 00:20:35.984
had. You know, I always had it within
my mother, but my mother was, my

00:20:35.984 --> 00:20:40.197
mother is, she's one of the strongest
women I ever came across, you know,

00:20:40.197 --> 00:20:44.368
so she always had that, you know. My
grandma was loving and caring, but she

00:20:44.368 --> 00:20:49.081
was always like, "All right, you mess up, I'mma whoop
your ass now," you know what I mean?
-Yeah, trust me, I know what you mean.

00:20:52.459 --> 00:20:55.379
- Yeah, so, you know, when I, when I got

00:20:57.214 --> 00:21:02.052
older and I had my daughter, and it
was somebody who needed to lean on me,

00:21:02.636 --> 00:21:08.684
it gave me that sense of love and care and
empathy, and it kinda knocked down

00:21:08.684 --> 00:21:12.437
and softened the wall that I, that I
had built up for so long, you know?

00:21:12.437 --> 00:21:16.775
So, that helped me, you
know, but it definitely should be

00:21:16.775 --> 00:21:19.278
other ways, and we should feel OK.

00:21:19.319 --> 00:21:22.781
Especially when you know you made
it through the rough times in life,

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:26.660
to understand that you're a
man that you never, never have

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:27.953
to doubt or question yourself.

00:21:28.412 --> 00:21:31.290
It should be OK to be
vulnerable because we need it.

00:21:32.582 --> 00:21:33.417
Mentally, we need it.
-  Yeah. Absolutely.

00:21:33.458 --> 00:21:33.834
Absolutely.

00:21:33.834 --> 00:21:35.335
And I think mentally we need it.

00:21:35.335 --> 00:21:36.295
The world needs it.

00:21:36.503 --> 00:21:37.254
Everybody needs it.

00:21:37.796 --> 00:21:39.923
Um, you've talked about your daughters.

00:21:40.924 --> 00:21:42.801
How are you going to talk to

00:21:43.552 --> 00:21:44.886
your daughters about being Black?

00:21:45.178 --> 00:21:49.474
- I just tell them how beautiful—you
know, one thing since day one, before they

00:21:49.474 --> 00:21:52.894
could talk I always expressed to my
daughters, understand how beautiful

00:21:52.894 --> 00:21:59.609
they are, how intelligent they are,
how, you know, confident they should be.

00:21:59.609 --> 00:22:03.196
How, never let nothing
negative knock them down.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:07.909
So, you know, so many, so many
positives that especially

00:22:07.909 --> 00:22:09.536
a Black woman needs to hear

00:22:10.245 --> 00:22:12.122
on a daily basis.
- [Taylor:] Mmhmm.

00:22:12.664 --> 00:22:19.504
- So their entire life is, they
got nothing from that from me,

00:22:19.504 --> 00:22:21.882
like, that's all I give
them every single day.

00:22:21.882 --> 00:22:23.175
So they understand that

00:22:24.259 --> 00:22:27.846
when some B.S. come their way,
it don't even faze them.

00:22:28.305 --> 00:22:32.392
You know, be strong Black women,
understand what you need to do, how you

00:22:32.392 --> 00:22:35.937
need to do it, but it's going to be obstacles
that you're going to need to face,

00:22:35.937 --> 00:22:41.610
and these, this—make sure you assess
it, understand it and proceed with

00:22:42.069 --> 00:22:45.280
however you need to proceed
with it, you know, and just giving

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:46.448
them an understanding of that.

00:22:46.865 --> 00:22:47.074
You know?

00:22:47.074 --> 00:22:50.911
So, as long as they're knowledgeable
of understanding the wrongs

00:22:50.911 --> 00:22:52.788
and rights of, of life,

00:22:53.121 --> 00:22:53.455
you know,

00:22:53.455 --> 00:22:58.126
I feel like I'll be OK
once they become older

00:22:58.126 --> 00:22:59.419
Black women on this earth.

00:22:59.795 --> 00:23:01.171
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:23:03.131 --> 00:23:09.012
I obviously saw those very powerful images
of you and Kendrick Lamar and Russell

00:23:09.012 --> 00:23:11.306
Westbrook out protesting in Compton.

00:23:11.306 --> 00:23:14.059
And I was able to talk to
Kendrick about it, but he's like,

00:23:14.059 --> 00:23:16.103
"You know, now I want action.

00:23:16.103 --> 00:23:20.774
I want to figure out what I can do
to help, you know, past protesting."

00:23:21.149 --> 00:23:24.486
But just seeing you guys out there,
I think meant a lot to so many

00:23:24.486 --> 00:23:27.155
people, but how did that come to be?

00:23:27.322 --> 00:23:31.243
Like, how did you three get together and
say, "OK, let's protest. Let's meet here"?

00:23:31.535 --> 00:23:32.327
Walk me through that.

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:35.497
- I don't, I don't, maybe people
didn't see it. It was a

00:23:35.497 --> 00:23:37.958
couple—probably about a week ago,

00:23:37.999 --> 00:23:42.629
it was a couple of
cops in Compton that had

00:23:43.505 --> 00:23:45.799
beat up a young Black male

00:23:46.675 --> 00:23:49.177
one night. And it was all over the news.

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:52.806
And it was one of those
moments to where I called,

00:23:52.806 --> 00:23:57.519
I actually called my high school
coach. And we was talking about it,

00:23:57.519 --> 00:24:00.689
and I was like, “Let's figure out
something we can do for the city,

00:24:00.856 --> 00:24:03.900
you know, to unite the city.”
Because at that time, Compton

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:08.947
hadn't had a protest yet. You
know, major parts of L.A. had.

00:24:09.656 --> 00:24:13.869
Um, and I just told him this is another
opportunity for us to be able to

00:24:13.869 --> 00:24:19.332
come together and show, you know,
one of the key cities in L.A. that

00:24:19.666 --> 00:24:21.668
we united in and it’s staying together.

00:24:22.252 --> 00:24:23.128
I fell asleep.

00:24:23.128 --> 00:24:24.254
I woke up the next day.

00:24:24.546 --> 00:24:26.715
My high school coach
had talked to the mayor.

00:24:27.382 --> 00:24:27.883
Umm.

00:24:28.383 --> 00:24:30.385
Next thing you know, we
put the protest together.

00:24:30.594 --> 00:24:31.887
Um, we put it out.

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:34.181
I'm sending it to everybody

00:24:34.181 --> 00:24:34.639
I knew.

00:24:35.557 --> 00:24:36.057
And we

00:24:37.184 --> 00:24:38.685
went to the protest.

00:24:38.727 --> 00:24:42.022
You know, Russ has been
one of the close friends of

00:24:42.022 --> 00:24:45.275
mine since I can remember.

00:24:45.484 --> 00:24:50.614
And he always been an advocate when
it comes to things like this—his father,

00:24:50.614 --> 00:24:55.327
his parents, everybody, you know,
has been one of those, one of those

00:24:55.327 --> 00:24:59.831
things. And Kendrick, I mean, he's
from Compton, you know? So it's,

00:25:00.999 --> 00:25:04.711
it was a no-brainer knowing, you know, he
was going to come and support. And,

00:25:05.128 --> 00:25:11.593
you know, Kendrick, you barely see Kendrick
these days.
- Yeah, I know. Yeah.
- For him to come out and

00:25:11.676 --> 00:25:13.261
show that and give that inspiration,

00:25:13.303 --> 00:25:19.601
it's great because Kendrick has been an
inspiration for, you know, this generation, kids

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.561
coming up in Compton.

00:25:21.561 --> 00:25:23.563
So, you know, it was a beautiful moment.

00:25:23.647 --> 00:25:29.027
A moment that I'll cherish for the
rest of my life, being able to be

00:25:29.027 --> 00:25:32.948
a part of that and giving whatever
positivity and inspiration I could to it.

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:33.990
- Yeah.

00:25:34.032 --> 00:25:40.539
No, it's very powerful for people
to see those that they look at often and

00:25:40.539 --> 00:25:42.457
look too often, you know, protesting.

00:25:42.457 --> 00:25:44.209
So, no, thank you absolutely

00:25:44.292 --> 00:25:45.043
for doing that.

00:25:45.502 --> 00:25:47.337
I put out a report

00:25:47.629 --> 00:25:51.550
recently talking about the Zoom
calls NBA players have been on.

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:54.386
I know you guys have already done
one, and there's about to be another

00:25:54.386 --> 00:25:58.181
Zoom call just about what you all
can do to unify and take a stand.
- [DeMar:] Mmhmm.

00:25:59.182 --> 00:26:02.352
- I assume you have, but have you been
on those Zoom calls, privy to them? And

00:26:02.352 --> 00:26:08.275
just tell me what they have been like.
- Very intense. I mean, people know us for being

00:26:08.316 --> 00:26:11.695
athletes but don't understand how
competitive we are when it comes to

00:26:11.945 --> 00:26:15.240
other things and how vocal, how intelligent

00:26:15.824 --> 00:26:18.410
every single guy that's
in this profession is.

00:26:19.077 --> 00:26:24.374
And every one of those
guys has incredible ideas,

00:26:24.916 --> 00:26:29.629
passion, will to want to put a
foot forward to making a change.

00:26:29.796 --> 00:26:32.465
You know, every step of
the way. You seen guys

00:26:33.091 --> 00:26:37.596
been doing it for years now. You
know, guys like Melo, CP, you know,

00:26:37.762 --> 00:26:43.560
Bron, D-Wade. It's all these guys that's
coming together and even teaching

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:48.648
and bringing up the young guys to
understand how to go about this thing.

00:26:48.648 --> 00:26:50.984
So it's definitely been
a passionate thing,

00:26:51.568 --> 00:26:55.071
in passionate conversations on how
to push this thing forward, you know.
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:59.200
- Whenever you can get a collective,
guys that's the ultimate competitors,

00:26:59.367 --> 00:27:00.702
and put their minds to something,

00:27:01.369 --> 00:27:03.246
I really believe something great
going to come out of it.

00:27:03.663 --> 00:27:07.709
- I've heard that Kyrie has been very vocal
in this and leading a lot of discussions.

00:27:07.709 --> 00:27:10.629
Are there any other guys that
you have, like, kind of seen

00:27:10.629 --> 00:27:13.423
emerge as leaders in this?
- Right,

00:27:13.423 --> 00:27:13.798
yeah,

00:27:13.882 --> 00:27:14.257
Yeah.

00:27:14.299 --> 00:27:15.175
You know, a lot of guys

00:27:16.635 --> 00:27:18.762
that's quiet, stay to themselves,

00:27:18.845 --> 00:27:22.349
you know, they, they stepping
forward, you know, putting

00:27:22.349 --> 00:27:25.560
a foot forward and understanding,
like, this is an opportunity for us

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:28.063
to really show who we really are.

00:27:28.271 --> 00:27:28.897
And you see it.

00:27:29.105 --> 00:27:34.944
You know, like I said, we know how a lot
of us are, but the world don't. And

00:27:35.362 --> 00:27:38.156
seeing these guys stepped forward,
showing their true character is

00:27:38.948 --> 00:27:40.450
going to be even more amazing.

00:27:41.493 --> 00:27:41.785
- I'm sure

00:27:41.785 --> 00:27:44.663
there have to be many more
conversations, but just in your

00:27:44.663 --> 00:27:49.000
opinion, what do you think will
happen and what do you want to happen?

00:27:49.501 --> 00:27:57.967
- Um, in my opinion, I think something
that hasn't been done, um, through,

00:27:58.259 --> 00:28:04.182
you know, all our leaders that set the
groundwork for changing, you know,

00:28:05.141 --> 00:28:09.145
equality and justice, just everything,

00:28:09.187 --> 00:28:14.567
I think it's finally, finally going
to be able to switch and

00:28:15.443 --> 00:28:16.653
lean to the right way.

00:28:16.695 --> 00:28:20.824
To fairness, to how it
shoulda always been, you know?

00:28:20.824 --> 00:28:26.079
And I think you definitely going to see
the world come together and all the

00:28:26.079 --> 00:28:31.209
people that's against this be exposed
and be outnumbered by all

00:28:31.209 --> 00:28:35.046
the greatness that everybody else is
trying to do for the next generation.

00:28:35.422 --> 00:28:38.925
So I really truly believe
that it will be a change.

00:28:39.384 --> 00:28:45.557
And it won't be another 10, 20,
30 years of this madness because

00:28:45.807 --> 00:28:55.108
of the power of the people stepping
forward with undeniably—proof.

00:28:55.567 --> 00:29:02.699
Like, you know?
- It's right there in front of you.

00:29:02.699 --> 00:29:08.163
- It's right there in front, it's there for us to
understand and take advantage of and run
with and put it out there to be fair.

00:29:10.123 --> 00:29:13.835
And I truly believe that. I truly
believe that.
- Popovich has been

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:16.296
such a voice throughout this.

00:29:16.337 --> 00:29:17.255
And always is.

00:29:17.255 --> 00:29:21.551
He always gives his thoughts on politics,
social justice, racial injustice.

00:29:22.177 --> 00:29:25.722
What conversations does he
have with you guys internally?

00:29:26.347 --> 00:29:31.519
Not just about racial issues, but
the role that white people play

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:42.155
in those issues.
- Man, since Day 1
I got with the Spurs, Pop

00:29:42.155 --> 00:29:46.409
was the most conscious person

00:29:46.409 --> 00:29:54.125
when it comes to society that I came across,
especially a white person, you know.

00:29:55.794 --> 00:30:00.715
I had the opportunity to play with
Dwane Casey, that's from Kentucky.

00:30:00.757 --> 00:30:04.594
He always used to express how
he grew up in the South, how it was, how

00:30:04.594 --> 00:30:06.221
segregation was and everything.

00:30:06.221 --> 00:30:11.851
And he always used to speak about,
you know, that injustice and all

00:30:11.851 --> 00:30:14.437
those things that came with, you
know, growing up back in the day.

00:30:15.021 --> 00:30:20.109
And I learned so much from him.
And the flip side, to go play

00:30:20.109 --> 00:30:22.570
with a coach, a white coach,

00:30:22.987 --> 00:30:24.239
that feels the same way,

00:30:24.322 --> 00:30:28.409
like he was a Black man, of
understanding the unfairness.

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:30.453
Um,

00:30:30.829 --> 00:30:31.120
how much

00:30:31.162 --> 00:30:36.960
things need to change. It's
Pop, man. It's been so many days

00:30:36.960 --> 00:30:41.548
where we had come in and we think
we have film, and Pop would sit us

00:30:41.548 --> 00:30:43.299
down and show us something about

00:30:44.175 --> 00:30:49.556
a crooked cop or a man
losing his life for nothing.

00:30:49.973 --> 00:30:54.477
How messed up the system is, how—just
all these things that's been going on.

00:30:54.519 --> 00:30:59.399
He been making us aware of this, even
if you wasn't paying attention or

00:30:59.566 --> 00:31:03.987
as passionate as the next person, he put
it on the forefront every single day.

00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:06.823
There wasn't a day that went
by where Pop does not,

00:31:07.407 --> 00:31:10.952
you know, so it's not surprised me when
I see him speak up and say these things.

00:31:11.452 --> 00:31:16.332
Behind closed doors, he'd do this on a
daily basis to show us how messed-up

00:31:16.749 --> 00:31:22.589
of a society that we live in and how
it's on us to change it, you know?

00:31:22.589 --> 00:31:30.179
- Your team meetings would be video.
- Yeah, he will, he will show a clip

00:31:31.014 --> 00:31:33.808
or whatever a topic is of something,

00:31:33.933 --> 00:31:36.769
and it will put life
in perspective for you.

00:31:37.437 --> 00:31:37.604
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:31:37.604 --> 00:31:40.773
- You know, and it's one of
the most incredible things

00:31:41.482 --> 00:31:45.737
that a lot of people wouldn't know
unless you play for him, that it

00:31:45.737 --> 00:31:51.701
definitely goes beyond basketball. And
to see a white man of his stature,

00:31:52.577 --> 00:31:55.830
he'd be the first one to jump out
there and he don't care what no

00:31:55.830 --> 00:32:00.335
one else thinks.
- [Taylor:] Mhmm.
- He speaks the truth, and you see it
in his words, you know.

00:32:00.335 --> 00:32:03.087
You gotta, you gotta love and
respect an individual like that.

00:32:03.463 --> 00:32:03.796
- Yeah.

00:32:03.838 --> 00:32:07.550
Well, when it comes to basketball,
obviously a lot of the talk has been,

00:32:08.009 --> 00:32:12.597
do players even want to play with all of
this injustice happening. In your opinion

00:32:12.597 --> 00:32:14.098
and what you've gathered from the calls,

00:32:14.641 --> 00:32:20.605
what makes people nervous about sitting
out? And what has that

00:32:20.605 --> 00:32:20.897
conversation been?

00:32:21.230 --> 00:32:27.403
- I think the conversation is, it's such
a tricky time that nobody, nobody

00:32:27.403 --> 00:32:33.952
expected or could foresee, with
the pandemic, with, you know,

00:32:34.118 --> 00:32:35.578
everything that's been going on,

00:32:35.662 --> 00:32:42.335
it's just been, it's just been a
tricky, tricky year so far, you know?

00:32:42.669 --> 00:32:43.086
And

00:32:45.296 --> 00:32:47.006
it's so many mixed feelings.

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:52.679
And in terms of, you know, how
different and difficult it's going

00:32:52.679 --> 00:32:56.975
to be, attempting to play, with everything
that's going on that's bigger than us,

00:32:57.058 --> 00:33:00.561
bigger than, bigger than basketball,
bigger than just playing a game,

00:33:01.813 --> 00:33:02.271
you know.

00:33:02.563 --> 00:33:06.609
So it's hard to shift all of a sudden
without playing the sports we love,

00:33:07.068 --> 00:33:08.361
with everything going on,

00:33:08.403 --> 00:33:13.282
to all of a sudden, our focus and our
mindset has been towards, you know, trying

00:33:13.282 --> 00:33:14.701
to make a change and make a difference.

00:33:15.076 --> 00:33:15.535
Umm.

00:33:16.119 --> 00:33:21.124
So it's definitely, it's definitely
mixed emotions, I think, to, we just

00:33:21.124 --> 00:33:26.337
trying to get to a place of settlement,
of understanding, like, OK, where

00:33:26.337 --> 00:33:30.508
can we put our energy to and still
be focused on other things at the

00:33:30.508 --> 00:33:34.303
same time. Because we, you know, we
still not sure how it's going to work

00:33:34.846 --> 00:33:40.893
when we go to Orlando, you know?
- [Taylor:] Right.
- There are still so many question marks—
- A lot.
- —of the not knowing

00:33:40.977 --> 00:33:43.187
and not feeling all the way comfortable.

00:33:43.187 --> 00:33:48.151
So, you know, just through human
nature, there'd definitely be hesitation

00:33:48.151 --> 00:33:51.070
there, you know, because you just
don't know, you know? It's not

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:53.406
like, aight, it's going back to regular.

00:33:53.406 --> 00:33:54.532
We playing in our arenas.

00:33:54.532 --> 00:33:55.324
We doing this.

00:33:55.366 --> 00:33:59.328
Now you saying we've been here for this
amount of time, locked in this place.

00:34:00.204 --> 00:34:01.497
All right, now you got to calm

00:34:01.998 --> 00:34:05.209
guys down of understanding
all that's going on.

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:08.087
How we going to do this?
- [Taylor:] Mmhmm.

00:34:08.379 --> 00:34:09.464
- Are we away from our families?

00:34:09.464 --> 00:34:13.217
So, like I said, there's so many
mixed emotions. But I know for a

00:34:13.217 --> 00:34:15.344
fact, guys want to play basketball.

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:19.182
You know, it's just, it's just trying
to get through all these emotions

00:34:19.557 --> 00:34:20.725
of things that's going on.

00:34:21.392 --> 00:34:27.690
- I will lastly—I end all of these with
the same question. And that is: Words mean

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:29.484
different things to different people.

00:34:30.151 --> 00:34:34.447
How do you define "America"?
- Being Black,

00:34:35.448 --> 00:34:38.576
you have to be strong enough to
bring up the next person behind you

00:34:38.951 --> 00:34:45.333
to be even greater, to make life
better for anyone to come after you.

00:34:45.666 --> 00:34:51.547
That's what America means to me. Being
Black, putting yourself in a position

00:34:51.547 --> 00:34:57.220
to be able to pull up somebody below you
to do something better than you, to make life

00:34:57.553 --> 00:34:59.972
much more better for the next person.

00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:01.599
That's,

00:35:02.683 --> 00:35:03.768
that's how I look at it.

